WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:08.374 align:start position:0% line:0%
Yo!

00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:09.776 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
Hi. Thanks
for meeting me here.

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.310 size:78% position:39% line:0%
This is your high school?

00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:13.046 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- This is my high school.
- What, uh--

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.481 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
Murry Bergtraum High School
for Business Careers.

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:17.483 align:start size:75% position:25%
As you can see,
we're directly next to--

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:21.788 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Across from the Tombs.
- Yeah, surrounded
  by One Police Plaza.

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:25.258 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, Bergtraum is viewed
as one of the worst schools
in the city.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:30.730 size:84% position:58%
Like, I had to kind of join
an elite group within this
high school to stay afloat.

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:35.168 align:start size:75% position:25%
I just felt like
most of the time,
I was trying to survive.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.670 align:start position:0%
Our meritocracy,
because it's based
on competition,

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:41.107 align:start position:0%
worships superiority
rather than excellence.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
Certain people get to start
way farther ahead.

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:49.148
Fabiola: I feel like for so much
of my education and childhood,

00:00:49.181 --> 00:00:52.819 align:start position:0%
it was just like,
studying hard for tests
specifically to prove

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:54.821 align:start position:0%
that I'm good enough
through tests.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:57.123
<i> Education is supposed
 to be an equalizer.</i>

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:59.725
<i> It is built
 on the meritocratic belief</i>

00:00:59.758 --> 00:01:02.562
<i> that effort and ability
 are the keys to success.</i>

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.731
<i>But is that really all it takes
 to be successful in school?</i>

00:01:05.764 --> 00:01:08.201 line:0%
<i> What about opportunity
 and access?</i>

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:11.204
This false notion
of a meritocracy

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:13.539
has furthered the interests

00:01:13.572 --> 00:01:15.842
of those of great wealth
in this country

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.443
and those of
great political power.

00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:19.846 align:start position:0%
So, why are we here?

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:23.216 size:88% position:44%
I'm on a quest to figure out
why the meritocratic beliefs

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.218 align:start position:0%
that the American dream
is built on

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.187
don't really work
for people who look like us.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.491 align:start position:0%
And so I want to figure out
how we got here

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:32.759
and what we will do about it.

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:38.865 line:0%
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.308
"Halls of hell at New York's
worst high school."

00:01:49.341 --> 00:01:53.579
This is a headline
about my high school,
published in March 2012.

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:55.248
This headline is super dramatic.

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.883 line:0%
- Just a little dramatic.
- ( Fabiola chuckles )

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:59.418 align:start position:0% line:0%
How did you end up
at Murry Bergtraum
in the first place?

00:01:59.451 --> 00:02:01.754 align:start size:72% position:28%
So, I had a pretty high
GPA in middle school.

00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:04.257
It was a selective middle school
that I had to test to get into.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.893
Uh, but in New York City,
you basically rank

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:08.895
what high schools
you'd be interested in going to,

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:10.730
and not a single school
selected me.

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:13.699
I got a call instead from,
like, the Department of Ed

00:02:13.732 --> 00:02:15.601
saying, "Hey, you gotta go
to second round."

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:16.903
And that's something
you don't want to do,

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:19.405
'cause second round,
what they did was

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:22.408
they handed you a list
of high schools and said,

00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:24.210
"These are the places
that have room."

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:27.446
And when you look at that list,
it's basically a list of schools

00:02:27.479 --> 00:02:28.915
that were considered
failing schools,

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:30.783
schools that were
plagued by violence,

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:32.552
just under-resourced.

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:35.288
And so I looked
at the list and was like,

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.657
"I'm gonna make a choice based
off of what's less bad."

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.792 align:start size:78% position:22%
That's crazy, though.
That's, like, med school.

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:41.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
- It's-- it's bad.
- You shouldn't have
  to match for high school.

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:43.462 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, it's bad.
- Christophe: Or middle school.

00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:46.432 align:end position:100%
I mean, the idea of
in elementary school

00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:48.801 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
having to test into
a competitive middle school

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:51.404 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- was completely not
  on my radar at the time.
- Fabiola: Exactly.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.472
So, the reason
why I have you here today

00:02:53.505 --> 00:02:57.410
is because
I'm exploring this question
is meritocracy a myth?

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.478 line:0%
Here we have a game
called Rigged,

00:02:59.511 --> 00:03:01.314 line:0%
and I think this
can help us illustrate

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:02.949
whether it's just
ability and effort

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:04.750
that allows you
to get successful.

00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:06.652 line:92%
The object of the game is to get

00:03:06.685 --> 00:03:08.354
to Glad You Asked University.

00:03:08.387 --> 00:03:09.956
Look how beautiful that is.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.491
There are two different sides
to the board game.

00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:14.493
So, we have Pleasantville
on that end

00:03:14.526 --> 00:03:16.963 line:0%
and then we have
Smallville over here.

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:19.332
So, I'm going to
have you pull a card

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.534
to determine essentially
what your fate is.

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:24.870 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Are you guys ready to play?
- I'm ready.

00:03:24.903 --> 00:03:28.341 align:start position:0%
All right.
My card says, "Home.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.576 align:start position:0%
You come from a two-parent
middle class household.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:33.412 align:start position:0%
Action: Move two steps
forward at Pleasantville."

00:03:33.445 --> 00:03:36.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Your mom
is a food service worker
and your dad is cab driver.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.784 align:start size:75% position:25%
Go start at Smallville."

00:03:38.817 --> 00:03:40.620 align:start position:0%
"You come from
a low-income household,

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.589 align:start position:0%
and I start from
the start at Smallville."

00:03:43.622 --> 00:03:45.291 align:start size:63% position:38%
Both of my parents
are lawyers,

00:03:45.324 --> 00:03:49.362
and guess how far
I get to move on this path.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.864
I get to move five steps
forward at Pleasantville.

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:55.001
One, two, three, four, five.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:58.871 align:start position:0%
"You've been selected
for the honor roll.
Advance three steps."

00:03:58.904 --> 00:04:00.873 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So, you got an opportunity.
- Opportunity!

00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:05.044 align:start size:97% position:3%
"You have won a scholarship
for being athlete of the year."

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:06.779 align:start size:91% position:9%
- You gotta be kidding me.
- ( laughter )

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:11.550 align:start position:0%
"You did not go to pre-K.
Pay $15,000 and skip a turn."

00:04:11.583 --> 00:04:13.686
Bill de Blasio, where you at?

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:15.455 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm already almost cooked.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:18.724 align:start position:0%
"Serendipity.
Your teacher recommends you

00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:20.993 size:84% position:42%
for a gifted and talented
program that you test into.

00:04:21.026 --> 00:04:23.663 align:start position:0%
Collect $10,000."

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:26.565
"You inherit $40,000
from your distant uncle.

00:04:26.598 --> 00:04:30.436
Take the next ladder."
Oh, my God.

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:31.738 align:start position:0%
I'm enjoying this ride.

00:04:33.772 --> 00:04:36.442 align:start size:59% position:41%
I can tell who
designed this game.

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:39.045 size:88% position:56%
"Your big brother was shot."

00:04:39.078 --> 00:04:40.946 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Whoa.
- Wow. Jesus.

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:42.882 align:start size:81% position:19%
"You miss two weeks
of school for bereavement.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:45.418 align:start size:69% position:31%
Pay the bank $10,000."

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.620 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Jeez.
- That's intense.

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:49.422 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah.
- "You can't do your homework

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:52.591 align:start position:0%
because the wifi is out.
Pay $5,000."

00:04:52.624 --> 00:04:54.593 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That's hard.
- I have done stories on that.

00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:55.828
- Yeah.
- Mm-hmm.

00:04:55.861 --> 00:04:57.763 align:start size:59% position:41%
"You start to doubt
your abilities

00:04:57.796 --> 00:04:59.598 align:start size:50% position:50%
as a student
in middle school

00:04:59.631 --> 00:05:01.967 align:start size:75% position:25%
so you begin to act out.
Take the next detour."

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.704
- So, you just gotta--
- Nothing's working out.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.972
You know, I mean,
this is what happens
when you're watching

00:05:07.005 --> 00:05:11.010
your kid siblings at night
and your mom's missing work.

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:12.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
- And you're tired when you
  get to school, you know?
- Yeah.

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:13.913
I mean, you talk back
to your teachers,

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:18.017
and then you can't escape
ol' Smallville.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.386 align:start size:91% position:9%
- All right.
- Fabiola's about to win.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:23.622
- She really is.
- Two steps. Wow!

00:05:23.655 --> 00:05:25.624
I think you guys
need to clap for me.

00:05:25.657 --> 00:05:28.094
Thank you so much.

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:30.363
So, is this game fair?

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:31.464 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Do you think this was fair?
- Obviously not.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:33.499 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Obviously not.
- Why not?

00:05:33.532 --> 00:05:36.402 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Because we lost.
- ( laughter )

00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:38.971 align:start size:75% position:25%
Clearly. Uh, no.
I mean, just by the fact

00:05:39.004 --> 00:05:44.009 align:start size:88% position:13%
that certain people get
to start way farther ahead
means that it can't be fair.

00:05:44.042 --> 00:05:47.880
Our positions are also exactly
following our family wealth.

00:05:47.913 --> 00:05:50.649 align:start position:0%
You can't escape
your neighborhood, like,
no matter what you do.

00:05:50.682 --> 00:05:51.984 align:start position:0%
The odds are just
stacked against you.

00:05:52.017 --> 00:05:54.520
This is incredibly frustrating.

00:05:54.553 --> 00:05:58.090 align:start size:63% position:38%
So, my question is
why is America so
obsessed with merit?

00:05:58.123 --> 00:06:02.661 align:start size:81% position:19%
Why is it such a huge part
of our country's fabric?

00:06:02.694 --> 00:06:06.132
<i> The concept of a meritocracy
 goes back centuries.</i>

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:08.934
<i> However, it took off
 in America in the 1950s</i>

00:06:08.967 --> 00:06:11.670
<i> due to dissatisfaction
 with an inept</i>

00:06:11.703 --> 00:06:14.874
<i> yet excessively wealthy
 aristocracy.</i>

00:06:14.907 --> 00:06:16.675
<i> In a quest
 to diversity the elite</i>

00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:19.512
<i> and increase social mobility
 for the working class,</i>

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:22.014 line:0%
<i> a group of reformers
 came together and decided</i>

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:24.683 line:0%
<i> that society must offer
 enough opportunity</i>

00:06:24.716 --> 00:06:26.652 line:0%
<i> for talent to combine
 with effort</i>

00:06:26.685 --> 00:06:29.789 line:0%
<i> so that anyone
 could rise to the top.</i>

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:34.193
<i> However, 1950s America
 was consumed by racism
 and segregation.</i>

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:38.531
<i> Black and Native American
 children were not taught
 with white children,</i>

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:41.033
<i> often had less access
 to resources,</i>

00:06:41.066 --> 00:06:43.702
<i> and were schooled
 in sub-standard buildings.</i>

00:06:43.735 --> 00:06:47.840
<i> Nevertheless,
the term meritocracy took hold,</i>

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:51.110
<i> and society embraced merit
 as the key way to unlocking
 the American dream.</i>

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:56.215 align:start size:78% position:22%
So, what is your reaction
just off the bat?

00:06:56.248 --> 00:06:58.117 align:start position:0% line:0%
You know, if you have
a connection

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.186 align:start position:0% line:0%
between race and class,
let's say,

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:02.721 align:start position:0% line:0%
and you exacerbate
economic inequality,

00:07:02.754 --> 00:07:04.190 align:start position:0% line:0%
you're gonna harm
people of color,

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:05.724 size:75% position:38%
even if you also benefit

00:07:05.757 --> 00:07:08.060 align:start position:0%
some people of color
whom you admit.

00:07:08.093 --> 00:07:12.164 align:start position:0%
Unless you're either
super talented or super lucky,

00:07:12.197 --> 00:07:13.732
if you're outside of the elite,

00:07:13.765 --> 00:07:15.601 align:start position:0%
probably hard work
is not gonna be enough.

00:07:15.634 --> 00:07:18.737 align:start position:0%
The new meritocrats,
who had themselves
gotten ahead

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:20.906 align:start position:0%
by being good in school
and good at tests,

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:25.211 align:start position:0%
had an almost unlimited appetite
for educating their children

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:27.913 align:start position:0%
and incredible
capacity to do so.

00:07:27.946 --> 00:07:32.751 align:start position:0%
And so today's meritocrats,
they spend resources
on training their kids

00:07:32.784 --> 00:07:35.154 align:start position:0%
that nobody else
in society can match.

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:38.224 align:start position:0% line:0%
If you go to the average
public school in America today,

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:41.026 align:start position:0% line:0%
probably about $12,000
to $15,000 a year

00:07:41.059 --> 00:07:43.229 align:start position:0% line:0%
is spent on educating you
per child.

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:46.765 align:start position:0% line:0%
If you go to one of the "Forbes"
top 20 private schools,

00:07:46.798 --> 00:07:50.236 align:start position:0% line:0%
probably about $75,000
is spent per child

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:52.238 size:84% position:42% line:0%
per year on educating kids.

00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:55.608 align:start position:0%
And so it's not just
poor people who can't keep up
with today's elite.

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:57.776
- It's the middle class.
- As part of this journey,

00:07:57.809 --> 00:08:01.113
I've been trying to understand
why we rely on tests so much

00:08:01.146 --> 00:08:02.781 align:start size:59% position:41%
and what they're
actually measuring.

00:08:02.814 --> 00:08:05.784 align:start position:0%
Our meritocracy,
because it's based
on competition,

00:08:05.817 --> 00:08:09.054 align:start position:0%
worships superiority
rather than excellence.

00:08:09.087 --> 00:08:13.259 align:start position:0%
And so the way
in which they measure

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:16.962 align:start position:0%
people's whatever,
"merit," is not neutral,

00:08:16.995 --> 00:08:20.799 align:start position:0%
particularly as between
white people and black people.

00:08:20.832 --> 00:08:24.803 align:start position:0%
If you have resources
to hire people whose job it is

00:08:24.836 --> 00:08:27.139 align:start position:0%
to teach your kids
how to do well at the tests,

00:08:27.172 --> 00:08:29.241 size:81% position:41%
you know, they're gonna do
really well at the test.

00:08:29.274 --> 00:08:32.578 align:start position:0%
So it's not just a matter
of outputs, but also inputs.

00:08:32.611 --> 00:08:35.814 align:start position:0%
What chances did you have,
and importantly,

00:08:35.847 --> 00:08:40.019 align:start position:0%
will society commit
to giving everybody
meaningful opportunity?

00:08:45.857 --> 00:08:50.296 align:start position:0%
How many tests
do you think the average
American kid takes

00:08:50.329 --> 00:08:52.998 size:88% position:44% line:0%
between grades K through 12?

00:08:53.031 --> 00:08:56.001 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
Well, I have a terrible memory,
but it makes sense to me

00:08:56.034 --> 00:08:58.837 align:start size:66% position:34%
that we probably took
maybe two a year.

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:03.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, from pre-K to senior year,
it would've been 28?

00:09:03.675 --> 00:09:05.978 align:start position:0%
Ooh. Okay, good guess.

00:09:06.011 --> 00:09:11.183 align:start position:0%
But I regret to say
that it is not that.

00:09:11.216 --> 00:09:14.320 align:start position:0%
It's actually 112.

00:09:14.353 --> 00:09:15.854
- No way.
- Yeah.

00:09:15.887 --> 00:09:18.023 align:start size:59% position:41%
Test-taking
is a specific skill

00:09:18.056 --> 00:09:21.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
- that is not like anything
  else we do in our lives.
- Yeah.

00:09:21.326 --> 00:09:23.095 align:start size:66% position:34%
And I really question
how much it transfers

00:09:23.128 --> 00:09:26.298 align:start size:63% position:38%
to actual, like,
things you need to
succeed as an adult.

00:09:26.331 --> 00:09:29.335 align:start position:0%
So, I want to explore
the tests that
we took growing up.

00:09:29.368 --> 00:09:32.271 align:start position:0%
I feel like this one
gets overlooked

00:09:32.304 --> 00:09:34.673 size:84% position:58%
because it's not mandatory.

00:09:34.706 --> 00:09:37.943
But G&amp;T, gifted and talented,
are you familiar with that?

00:09:37.976 --> 00:09:41.347 align:start size:69% position:31%
I am. Yeah. I mean,
we didn't call it that
in Arizona, though.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:45.384 align:start size:78% position:22%
We had a program
in elementary school
called Project Potential.

00:09:45.417 --> 00:09:49.355 align:start position:0%
These names are so--
as I'm older, thinking about
these names, I'm like,

00:09:49.388 --> 00:09:51.256 size:84% position:42%
they weren't trying to hide
what their motives were.

00:09:51.289 --> 00:09:53.025 align:start size:97% position:3%
- No. Yeah.
- Like, "This is what it is."

00:09:53.058 --> 00:09:56.829 align:start size:91% position:9%
The teacher would refer
certain students for testing,

00:09:56.862 --> 00:10:00.699 align:start size:63% position:38%
which in retrospect
gives a lot of power
to that teacher.

00:10:00.732 --> 00:10:02.167
Fabiola:
<i> You're right.</i>

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.370
<i> A 2016 study
 found that a teacher's race</i>

00:10:04.403 --> 00:10:07.072
<i> and racial bias
 consistently affected</i>

00:10:07.105 --> 00:10:10.743
<i> how they interpreted
 their students' behaviors
 or abilities</i>

00:10:10.776 --> 00:10:13.746
<i> and who they would recommend
 for a gifted program.</i>

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:16.081
<i> Additionally, black students
 are more likely</i>

00:10:16.114 --> 00:10:18.817
<i> than their white peers
 to receive suspensions,</i>

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:21.253
<i> be excluded
 for greater periods of time,</i>

00:10:21.286 --> 00:10:24.089
<i> and be disciplined
 for less serious offenses.</i>

00:10:24.122 --> 00:10:28.794
And we see the same kind of bias
in testing with the SHSAT.

00:10:28.827 --> 00:10:32.398 align:start position:0%
In New York City,
the SHSAT was the test
that you had to take

00:10:32.431 --> 00:10:34.433 align:start position:0%
in order to get into
a specialized high school.

00:10:34.466 --> 00:10:36.101 size:88% position:44%
If you can't pass this test,

00:10:36.134 --> 00:10:39.104 align:start position:0%
sorry, you can't, like,
go to one of these top schools.

00:10:39.137 --> 00:10:42.341
So, Stuyvesant, New York City's
most selective public school,

00:10:42.374 --> 00:10:47.112 align:start position:0%
in 2019, out of a class
of almost 900 students,

00:10:47.145 --> 00:10:49.281
only seven black kids got in.

00:10:49.314 --> 00:10:52.918
<i> In 2019, the New York City
 Independent Budget Office</i>

00:10:52.951 --> 00:10:55.954
<i> simulated what offers
to the incoming 9th grade class</i>

00:10:55.987 --> 00:10:58.290
<i> would've looked like
 if admissions were based</i>

00:10:58.323 --> 00:11:01.960
<i> on students' ranking
 in the top seven percent
 of their middle school</i>

00:11:01.993 --> 00:11:04.863
<i> instead of their ranking
 on the SHSAT.</i>

00:11:04.896 --> 00:11:07.966
<i> The report found that
 about 19% of admissions offers</i>

00:11:07.999 --> 00:11:09.802
<i> would have gone
 to black students,</i>

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:12.304
<i> and about 27 would have
 gone to Hispanic students,</i>

00:11:12.337 --> 00:11:16.975
<i> compared to the actual 4%
 and 6% respectively.</i>

00:11:17.008 --> 00:11:19.978
It seems like testing doesn't
happen in a bubble, right?

00:11:20.011 --> 00:11:22.448 align:start position:0%
It depends on how much money
your parents have.

00:11:22.481 --> 00:11:24.983
It depends on where you live.

00:11:25.016 --> 00:11:28.153 align:start position:0%
All of these things impact
what kinds of resources

00:11:28.186 --> 00:11:31.990
you're able to access inside
and outside of the classroom.

00:11:32.023 --> 00:11:37.162
I think the one that
hasn't really entered our
conversation yet is the SAT.

00:11:37.195 --> 00:11:38.831
Joss:
Ah, yes.

00:11:38.864 --> 00:11:42.835 align:start position:0%
Yeah, did you know that
the SAT was founded by a guy

00:11:42.868 --> 00:11:44.503 align:start position:0%
who supported
the eugenics movement?

00:11:44.536 --> 00:11:48.507
- No. That's deeply disturbing.
- Yeah, it's very disturbing.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.310
This is data straight
from College Board,

00:11:51.343 --> 00:11:53.178 size:81% position:41%
which administers the SAT.

00:11:53.211 --> 00:11:58.484 align:start position:0%
In 2015, 1.7 million students
sat for the SAT.

00:11:58.517 --> 00:12:02.020
What stands out
probably the most is
for black test-takers,

00:12:02.053 --> 00:12:04.189
we see the lowest scores
across the board,

00:12:04.222 --> 00:12:06.492
and the lowest in writing.

00:12:06.525 --> 00:12:09.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's not just how well
you prepare for this one test,

00:12:09.394 --> 00:12:12.030 align:start size:63% position:38%
but how well your
entire education has
prepared you for it,

00:12:12.063 --> 00:12:14.466 align:start size:81% position:19%
and we know that
the schools are not equal.

00:12:14.499 --> 00:12:16.502 align:start position:0%
For me,
I was fortunate enough

00:12:16.535 --> 00:12:18.370 align:start position:0%
through a program
called Legal Outreach

00:12:18.403 --> 00:12:21.340 align:start position:0%
to take an entire summer
of SAT prep course.

00:12:21.373 --> 00:12:23.041 align:start position:0%
Reflecting on
that opportunity,

00:12:23.074 --> 00:12:25.210 align:start position:0%
I was like,
"Wow, I had to apply, again,

00:12:25.243 --> 00:12:26.812 size:81% position:41%
to get into that program."

00:12:26.845 --> 00:12:32.217 align:start position:0%
I may have a 95 GPA,
but these programs

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:36.021 align:start position:0%
kind of helped me
understand how to play
the game of the system.

00:12:36.054 --> 00:12:37.890
So, how was the test for you?

00:12:37.923 --> 00:12:39.391 align:start position:0%
The test? I actually--

00:12:39.424 --> 00:12:42.094 align:start position:0%
I think because of the prep,
I enjoyed taking the SAT.

00:12:42.127 --> 00:12:46.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
- With reading and writing,
  like, I did really well.
- You're a full nerd.

00:12:46.097 --> 00:12:48.901 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Yeah, I'm just, like,
  into that kind of thing.
- You enjoy testing.

00:12:48.934 --> 00:12:51.403 align:start position:0%
There is one specific story
that I want to get into

00:12:51.436 --> 00:12:55.541 align:start position:0%
that I think helped shape
my idea of ability

00:12:55.574 --> 00:12:58.877 size:84% position:42%
and what it means to feel
special through the system.

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:02.381
My sister Grace,
who's two years older than me,

00:13:02.414 --> 00:13:05.851 align:start position:0%
got into a program
in New York City
called Prep for Prep.

00:13:05.884 --> 00:13:10.355
That program
basically identifies
the high-achieving,

00:13:10.388 --> 00:13:12.491
low-income students
across the city

00:13:12.524 --> 00:13:15.561 align:start position:0%
and says, "We want to help
train these students

00:13:15.594 --> 00:13:18.263 align:start position:0%
by giving them extra
course work to prepare them

00:13:18.296 --> 00:13:21.433 align:start position:0%
for some of the best
private schools in America."

00:13:21.466 --> 00:13:23.569
So, Grace ended up
going to Exeter,

00:13:23.602 --> 00:13:25.604
then got into Princeton.
This is her Princeton diploma.

00:13:25.637 --> 00:13:28.841 align:start position:0%
Thank you, Grace,
for letting me have
this here today.

00:13:28.874 --> 00:13:31.143
My sister and I did very well.

00:13:31.176 --> 00:13:35.447 align:start position:0%
We are low-income kids,
grew up in Brooklyn, New York,

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:39.384
and clearly meritocracy to
some extent has worked for us

00:13:39.417 --> 00:13:42.454 align:start position:0%
because we were able
to take advantage
of opportunities

00:13:42.487 --> 00:13:45.057 align:start position:0%
like Prep for Prep,
like Legal Outreach,

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:47.292 align:start position:0%
like the Posse Foundation,
which by the way

00:13:47.325 --> 00:13:49.895 align:start position:0%
gave me a full tuition
scholarship to Dickenson.

00:13:49.928 --> 00:13:53.465 align:start position:0%
But I'm conflicted because
I'm the exception.

00:13:53.498 --> 00:13:55.968 align:start position:0%
Like, should I be up here
talking about this?

00:13:56.001 --> 00:13:59.438 align:start position:0%
Should I be talking
about my sister when
we are the exceptions?

00:13:59.471 --> 00:14:03.141 align:start position:0%
I wish that there
were a way to actually
level the playing field

00:14:03.174 --> 00:14:07.313 size:84% position:42%
so that everyone gets help,
everyone gets opportunity.

00:14:14.419 --> 00:14:17.322 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
- Fabiola: Hey, sis.
- Hey, sister.

00:14:17.355 --> 00:14:19.391 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
- What's going on?
- Nothing much.

00:14:19.424 --> 00:14:23.328 align:start size:81% position:19%
Just calling you as I work
through this episode.

00:14:23.361 --> 00:14:26.164
Thank you again for letting
me use your diplomas.

00:14:26.197 --> 00:14:28.133
As I'm working on this episode,

00:14:28.166 --> 00:14:30.335 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm conflicted, right?
Because it's worked out for us.

00:14:30.368 --> 00:14:32.504
It's helped a lot of people
in our family,

00:14:32.537 --> 00:14:36.041 align:start size:66% position:34%
but at the same time,
meritocracy has kept
a lot of people back.

00:14:36.074 --> 00:14:37.643 align:start size:72% position:28%
Poor people,
black and brown people,

00:14:37.676 --> 00:14:39.912 align:start size:56% position:44%
they're still
not getting ahead.

00:14:39.945 --> 00:14:43.548
Yeah, that's something
I think about as well.

00:14:43.581 --> 00:14:47.119
Our accomplishments
are definitely celebrated,

00:14:47.152 --> 00:14:50.355
but at the same time,
they're weaponized

00:14:50.388 --> 00:14:53.659
and used against
people who look like us
to pretty much say,

00:14:53.692 --> 00:14:56.428
"If they can do it,
why can't you do the same?

00:14:56.461 --> 00:15:00.032
But they're forgetting
the fact that ultimately

00:15:00.065 --> 00:15:04.069
we always just had to do
way more with a lot less.

00:15:04.102 --> 00:15:05.537 align:start size:63% position:38%
So, there's actually
an organization,

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:07.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're called
the Brotherhood/Sister Sol,

00:15:07.706 --> 00:15:10.208
and they've been working
to level the playing field.

00:15:10.241 --> 00:15:12.544
They've been giving students
more opportunities

00:15:12.577 --> 00:15:15.013
because they realize
that it's just not fair,

00:15:15.046 --> 00:15:16.682 align:start size:53% position:47%
so I want to try
and talk to them.

00:15:16.715 --> 00:15:18.517 size:84% position:58%
I'm gonna reach out and see

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:20.552 align:start size:78% position:22%
if they can help me
wrap my mind around this.

00:15:20.585 --> 00:15:22.554 align:start position:0%
Cool, cool.
Let me know how it goes, sis.

00:15:22.587 --> 00:15:26.358 align:start size:72% position:28%
I will, thank you. Bye.

00:15:34.699 --> 00:15:37.402
Man: You see our college
pendants up on the ceiling,

00:15:37.435 --> 00:15:40.405 size:81% position:59%
which is the universities
and colleges where our
young people are studying.

00:15:40.438 --> 00:15:44.242 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
And, you know,
it inspires the next generation
that comes in to see,

00:15:44.275 --> 00:15:45.577 align:start size:72% position:28% line:0%
you know,
what are opportunities.

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:47.245 line:0%
We take them on
college tours as well,

00:15:47.278 --> 00:15:49.147 line:0%
but this is
just a daily reminder.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:51.616 align:start position:0%
And are the students bringing
these back themselves?

00:15:51.649 --> 00:15:53.251
Yeah, they're always proud,
especially if they're

00:15:53.284 --> 00:15:54.586
the first to go to a school,
to put it up.

00:15:54.619 --> 00:15:56.321
Fabiola: Okay.

00:15:56.354 --> 00:15:58.523 align:start size:69% position:31%
Previously, we were
looking at SAT results

00:15:58.556 --> 00:16:02.294 align:start size:59% position:41%
and we saw
that black students
did not as well

00:16:02.327 --> 00:16:05.197 align:start size:72% position:28%
as their white
and Asian counterparts.

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:06.732
What's your response to that,

00:16:06.765 --> 00:16:09.201 align:start size:91% position:9%
and also would you
call that an achievement gap?

00:16:09.234 --> 00:16:11.603
I think there's a fundamental
access gap in the country.

00:16:11.636 --> 00:16:13.538 size:88% position:44%
It's not an achievement gap.

00:16:13.571 --> 00:16:15.273 size:75% position:38%
You can't have a country

00:16:15.306 --> 00:16:17.275 align:start position:0%
that has 13 generations
of slavery,

00:16:17.308 --> 00:16:20.078 align:start position:0%
when it was illegal
to read and write,

00:16:20.111 --> 00:16:22.581 align:start position:0%
four generations
of segregation,

00:16:22.614 --> 00:16:26.451 align:start position:0%
when the system
was by law unequal,

00:16:26.484 --> 00:16:29.554 align:start position:0%
and two generations
of legal freedom,

00:16:29.587 --> 00:16:31.757 align:start position:0%
and expect
the playing field to be equal.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:34.626 align:start position:0%
Until we create
a true equal playing field,

00:16:34.659 --> 00:16:37.529 align:start position:0%
you will not have
equal achievement.

00:16:37.562 --> 00:16:40.332 align:start position:0%
And we have to look
at access and opportunity.

00:16:40.365 --> 00:16:42.467 align:start position:0%
Those are the key
inflection points,

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.636 align:start position:0%
and I think that's what
we have to talk about

00:16:44.669 --> 00:16:46.405 align:start position:0%
if we really want
an equitable society.

00:16:46.438 --> 00:16:47.773 size:84% position:58%
When I was in eighth grade,

00:16:47.806 --> 00:16:49.775 align:start size:69% position:31%
I entered a program
called Legal Outreach.

00:16:49.808 --> 00:16:52.644 align:start size:78% position:22%
But I had to, like,
show what my grades were,

00:16:52.677 --> 00:16:55.781 align:start size:72% position:28%
and you couldn't have
below a certain average
to be in the program.

00:16:55.814 --> 00:16:57.649 align:start size:88% position:13%
Can you talk about
this idea of cherry-picking,

00:16:57.682 --> 00:17:00.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
and do you have the same
kind of philosophy at Bro/Sis?

00:17:00.452 --> 00:17:02.487 align:start position:0%
We do no sifting here.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:04.790 align:start position:0%
What we mean by that
is you can have any
grade point average

00:17:04.823 --> 00:17:06.658 size:81% position:41%
and join the organization.

00:17:06.691 --> 00:17:10.328 align:start position:0%
I think the role of education
should be focused

00:17:10.361 --> 00:17:13.498 align:start position:0%
on the development
of the whole child.

00:17:13.531 --> 00:17:15.667 align:start position:0%
It's really
about wraparound support
for young people

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:18.737 align:start position:0%
so that they are supported
and guided and loved,

00:17:18.770 --> 00:17:21.339 align:start position:0%
but that they also have
opportunities and access

00:17:21.372 --> 00:17:23.608 size:78% position:39%
so that they can develop
their own agency in life.

00:17:23.641 --> 00:17:27.179 align:start size:84% position:16%
In thinking about
the public school system
right now in New York City,

00:17:27.212 --> 00:17:30.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
what is it gonna take
to make it a real meritocracy?

00:17:30.782 --> 00:17:35.187 align:start position:0%
New York City
has a public school system
that is a caste system,

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.355 align:start position:0%
where you have elite schools,
good schools,

00:17:37.388 --> 00:17:39.357 align:start position:0%
mediocre schools,
and horrible schools.

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:42.527 align:start position:0%
And where you go
depends on your zip code
or the access,

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.830 align:start position:0%
either economic or cultural,
of your family.

00:17:44.863 --> 00:17:47.365 align:start position:0%
One of the reasons that
the public school system

00:17:47.398 --> 00:17:50.235 align:start position:0%
has been allowed
to flounder for so long

00:17:50.268 --> 00:17:53.438 align:start position:0%
is that it's overwhelmingly
serving low-income
black and brown kids.

00:17:53.471 --> 00:17:56.141 align:start position:0%
We have students
in the New York City
Public School system

00:17:56.174 --> 00:18:00.378 align:start position:0%
still today who don't
have access to computers
and technology at home,

00:18:00.411 --> 00:18:02.380 align:start position:0%
who don't have access
to wifi at home.

00:18:02.413 --> 00:18:04.816 size:81% position:41%
The school system has not
been able to provide that,

00:18:04.849 --> 00:18:08.553 align:start position:0%
so we at Brotherhood/Sister Sol
have provided that for
all of our young people.

00:18:08.586 --> 00:18:11.223 align:start position:0%
So, COVID is just
accentuating all of these

00:18:11.256 --> 00:18:13.725 align:start position:0%
existing realities
that were already here,

00:18:13.758 --> 00:18:16.394 align:start position:0%
and you see it
in the educational system,
you see it in the labor force,

00:18:16.427 --> 00:18:18.697 align:start position:0%
you see it in
the housing situation as well.

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:22.334 align:start position:0%
The non-profit sector,
which is a huge sector
in New York,

00:18:22.367 --> 00:18:27.572 align:start position:0%
is expected to do the work
that government has abdicated.

00:18:27.605 --> 00:18:29.875 align:start position:0%
Really,
at the end of the day,
we have to change

00:18:29.908 --> 00:18:34.246 align:start position:0%
the entire narrative
of what is acceptable
in this country,

00:18:34.279 --> 00:18:37.449 align:start position:0%
what true opportunity, equity,
and justice looks like.

00:18:42.754 --> 00:18:45.657
Fabiola:<i> It is clear
 that meritocracy is a myth.</i>

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:47.425
<i> And it's a dangerous one,</i>

00:18:47.458 --> 00:18:50.428
<i> because it
 legitimizes inequality.</i>

00:18:50.461 --> 00:18:52.764
<i> Education was supposed to be
 the driving force</i>

00:18:52.797 --> 00:18:56.701
<i> behind social mobility
 and racial integration.</i>

00:18:56.734 --> 00:19:00.939
<i> It was supposed to help us all
 achieve the American dream.</i>

00:19:00.972 --> 00:19:05.410
But until America can give us
all an equal chance at success--

00:19:05.443 --> 00:19:09.214
that includes
an equal starting point
and equal resources--

00:19:09.247 --> 00:19:12.784
America can never be
a meritocracy.

00:19:12.817 --> 00:19:14.553
As long as we define merit

00:19:14.586 --> 00:19:18.323
according to the whims
of winners,

00:19:18.356 --> 00:19:19.758
we'll always fall short.

00:19:19.791 --> 00:19:22.694
What's always been missing
from the equation...

00:19:24.529 --> 00:19:27.632
<i> ...are opportunities</i>

00:19:27.665 --> 00:19:29.935
and social good.

00:19:38.676 --> 00:19:40.412 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
Take one, mark.

00:19:40.445 --> 00:19:42.847 line:0%
♪ There's no such thing as cold,
no such thing as cold ♪

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:46.251 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- Oh, my God, I broke it.
- Oh, no.

00:19:46.284 --> 00:19:49.888 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- I'm sorry.
- Can we have some glue?

00:19:49.921 --> 00:19:51.823 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- You just collected.
- Lee: You just collected.

00:19:51.856 --> 00:19:54.226 line:0%
You were supposed to pay.
You just robbed the bank.

